<!--
  This is an EXAMPLE. Copy it to `CONTEXT.md` in this project root to take it into use;
  nothing reads this file at launch. `CONTEXT.md` is the operating contract for every
  agent in the session - the main agent and each subagent it starts - staged into
  `~/.kimi-code/AGENTS.md` and injected into all of them. It is where behaviour belongs,
  not voice. Tone, language, and reply shape belong in `SYSTEM.md`, which the main agent
  alone receives.

  Three states:

    no CONTEXT.md  this harness's own contract is used, from `runtime/AGENTS.md`. That is
                   the default and it is a real file with real authority, not a stub.
    CONTEXT.md     whatever you write here, instead. The harness contract is then not
                   loaded at all - this replaces it, it does not extend it.
    empty          no contract for anyone. Rarely what you want, and worth saying: the
                   project's own `AGENTS.md` still arrives alongside this, and you cannot
                   switch that one off from here.

  Template values. Two things read this text, and they substitute different names:

    ${harness.date}   filled in by the launcher at staging, so it costs nothing to use
    ${cwd} ${os} ...  filled in by Kimi Code in a system prompt, NOT here. Writing one of
                      them in this file puts the literal characters in front of the model.
                      `${agents_md}` is the one to be careful of: it names this very
                      document, so referencing it from inside the document produces a
                      sentence about itself rather than the contract.

  HTML comments like this one are stripped at staging and never reach the model, so a
  file you maintain can explain itself for free.
-->

# Project operating contract

## What this workspace is

The project here is built and tested as described in its own `AGENTS.md`, which arrives
with this contract and outranks nothing in it. Where the two disagree about how a task
is done in this repository, follow the project.

## How to work here

State your result in terms a reviewer can check. Staged on ${harness.date}.
